  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three complementary ways of mapping the seafloor, highlighting the tradeoff between coverage and resolution. From space, satellite altimetry measures sea surface height. Small bumps and dips in the sea surface, caused by variations in gravity from underwater features, indicate broad seafloor structure indirectly. This method covers large areas but has a wide footprint and relatively coarse mapping resolution.\n\nCloser to the ocean, a surface vessel maps the bottom directly with acoustic swaths. Its multibeam echo sounder produces a broad cone-shaped coverage area, labeled with swath width and footprint, showing that ships can map more detail than satellites while still surveying substantial areas.\n\nAt the highest resolution, autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs) fly near the seabed carrying MBES instruments. Because they are much closer to the bottom, their sonar footprint is much smaller, so they produce finer-scale maps, though over narrower swaths.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a hierarchy of seafloor mapping methods: satellites provide indirect, large-scale context; ships provide intermediate-resolution direct mapping; and AUVs provide the most detailed local surveys.",
